<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="activity-11-ODE_models2">
    <title>11 - Coding ODE Models Part 2</title>

    <!-- Exercises start here. -->
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let's return to our "simple" predator-prey model, where
                <m>x(t)</m> is the population of the prey and <m>y(t)</m> is the
                population of predators, at time <m>t</m>.
                Consider the following assumptions:
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        Left alone, prey will increase at a constant rate
                        <m>a</m>.
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        Prey die at a rate that is proportional to the number of
                        predators in the ecosystem.
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        Predators have evolved and reproduce when food is
                        plentiful, but don't reproduce when food is scarce,
                        i.e., predators grow proportionally to the number of
                        prey.
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        Predators die off at a rate proportional to the number
                        of predators (e.g., territorial).
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>

            <p>
                The corresponding model is given by two differential equations:
                <md>
                    \frac{dx}{dt} = a - by \\
                \frac{dy}{dt} = cx - dy \\
                </md>
                with initial conditions <m>x(0) = x_0</m> and <m>y(0) = y_0</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve the model numerically using Python.
                    Let the parameters be the following:
                    <m>{a = 5, b = 1,
                    c = 1, d = 0.2.}</m>
                    Additionally, the initial conditions are
                    <m>x_0 = 10, y_0 = 10</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="11-solutions">
                <program language="python">
                    <code>
import math
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
from scipy.integrate import odeint

### Parameters
a = 5
b = 1
c = 1
d = .2

x0 = 10    # Initial Prey (in hundreds)
y0 = 10    # Initial Predators (in hundreds)

#Define ODE
def model(z,t,a,b,c,d):          # (depend, independ, param), z = [x,y]
    x = z[0]             # First entry of z is x (prey)
    y = z[1]             # Second entry of z is y (predator)
    dxdt = a-b*y
    dydt = c*x - d*y
    return [dxdt, dydt]  # VECTOR [prey, predator]

endtime = 100             # define end time - easier to change later
t=np.linspace(0,endtime,endtime*20)

z0 = [x0, y0]            # initial values, in vector form [prey, predator]

## Find the solution, which is a vector z(t) = [x(t), y(t)]

z = odeint(model,z0,t,args=(a,b,c,d))   # Solution

# Plot both solutions
fig0 = plt.figure(0)
plt.plot(t,z[:,0], label='prey')        # x(t) = z[all rows, 1st column (prey)]
plt.plot(t,z[:,1], label='predators')   # y(t) = z[all rows, 2nd column (predators)]
plt.xlabel('time')
plt.ylabel('Population in hundreds')
plt.title('Super Simple Predator-Prey Model')
plt.legend()
plt.show()

# Plot y vs. x along with the fixed point
plt.figure(1)
plt.plot(z[:,0],z[:,1], 'r-', label='solution')   # x(t) = z[all rows, 1st column], y(t) = z[all rows, 2nd column]
plt.plot(a*d/(c*b), a/b, 'o', label='fixed point')  # fixed point
plt.xlabel('prey')
plt.ylabel('predators')
plt.legend()
plt.show()
                    </code>
                </program>

                <image source="images/11-PredPrey.png" width="75%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Graph of both predator and prey solution curves for the
                        simple Predator-Prey model.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the fixed point of the model.
                    Plot <m>y</m> vs.
                    <m>x</m> along with the fixed point.
                    What do you notice?
                    Comment on whether you think the fixed point is stable or
                    unstable.
                    Explain.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="11-solutions">
                <p>
                    The following is a continuation of the code for Part (a).
                    Note that the fixed point is represented generically - this
                    can help if you end up changing parameters.
                </p>

                <program language="python">
                    <code>
# Plot y vs. x along with the fixed point
plt.figure(1)
plt.plot(z[:,0],z[:,1], 'r-', label='solution')   # x(t) = z[all rows, 1st column], y(t) = z[all rows, 2nd column]
plt.plot(a*d/(c*b), a/b, 'o', label='fixed point')  # fixed point
plt.xlabel('prey')
plt.ylabel('predators')
plt.legend()
plt.show()
                    </code>
                </program>

                <image source="images/11-FixedPoint_PredPrey.png" width="75%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Fixed point and predator versus prey solution curves for
                        the simple Predator-Prey model.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>

                <p>
                    The fixed point sits at the center of a spiral curve for the
                    predators plotted versus the prey.
                    The solution spirals into this point, suggesting that it is
                    a stable fixed point.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What assumptions could you change to make this model
                    <q>better</q> or more realistic?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="11-solutions">
                <p>
                    This isn't quite realistic because of the negative
                    populations that occur.
                    One option is to change the initial conditions - should both
                    predator and prey start at the same population size?
                    Is that realistic?
                    Another option is to change certain parameters.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let's plot the 2D phase portrait and see if it aligns with
                    our conclusions in Part (b).
                    Consider the following code.
                    Will any initial conditions end at this fixed point?
                    Manipulate the phase portrait as needed to help justify your
                    answer.
                    Note: the code below relies on you defining your ODE as
                    <c>model</c>, and the output to the model being the solution
                    <c>z = [x(t), y(t)]</c>.
                </p>

                <program language="python">
                    <code>
import math
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
from scipy.integrate import odeint

### PHASE PORTRAIT
xdomain = np.linspace(-7.5,10,10)     # set up points for x-axis (prey)
ydomain = np.linspace(-10,15,10)       # set up points for y-axis (predators)

X,Y = np.meshgrid(xdomain,ydomain)    # creates 2D grid of all points to find the derivative vector

### We want [dxdt, dydt] at each point in our grid so:
# U = a-b*Y = dx/dt
# V = c*X - d*Y = dy/dt
[U, V] = model([X,Y],0,a,b,c,d)       # runs each point in the grid through the ODE to find the derivative vector at that point

plt.quiver(xdomain,ydomain,U,V,label='Phase portrait')      # plot phase portrait (arrows)
plt.plot(z[:,0],z[:,1],'r-', label='[10,10] initial soln')  # Plot the one time solution in red.
plt.plot(a*d/(c*b), a/b, 'o', label='fixed point')          # Plot the fixed point
plt.xlabel('prey')
plt.ylabel('predators')
plt.title('Phase Portrait')
plt.legend()
plt.show()
                    </code>
                </program>
            </statement>

            <solution component="11-solutions">
                <image source="images/11-PhasePortrait_PredPrey.png" width="75%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Phase portrait for the simple Predator-Prey model.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>

                <p>
                    This is a spiral sink, which is a stable fixed point.
                    Any initial condition will end at this fixed point.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>